% ============================================================================
\begin{abstract}
We present an end-to-end study of latent generative models for 48-frame
day-ahead nowcasting of {INSAT-3S} satellite imagery over the Indian
subcontinent. Two complete pipelines were implemented and compared on
identical data ($\sim$30~days, two channels: visible reflectance~(VIS) and
water-vapour brightness temperature~(WV)): a \emph{flow-matching} system
(\textsc{FlowCast}) using the {SEVIR}-pretrained \texttt{AutoencoderKL} frozen
together with a Conditional Flow Matching forecaster~\citep{flowcast2026,
lipman2023flowmatching}, and a \emph{diffusion} system (\textsc{DiffCast})
that additionally fine-tunes the {VAE} encoder and replaces the forecaster
with an {EDM}-style latent diffusion model~\citep{karras2022edm}. Both
systems share the same {Earthformer-UNet} backbone~\citep{gao2022earthformer}
and identical evaluation protocol, enabling a controlled architectural
comparison. We introduce a three-way ensemble aggregation
(pixel-mean / medoid / latent-mean) to expose the perception-distortion
tradeoff~\citep{blau2018perception} and explicitly characterise the
sharpness-versus-skill behaviour of each model. On VIS, \textsc{FlowCast}
(with Strategy~B decoder fine-tune) attains CSI$_M$~$=$~0.064,
SSIM~$=$~0.340, while \textsc{DiffCast} attains
CSI$_M$~$=$~0.012 with a higher PSNR of 15.25\,dB (vs.\ 12.02\,dB) and lower
CRPS (0.123 vs.\ 0.213). We trace \textsc{DiffCast}'s low CSI and visible
loss of geographic structure to an \emph{encoder representation collapse}
induced by end-to-end {VAE} fine-tuning on the small dataset.
\textsc{FlowCast}'s frozen-encoder design avoids this failure mode and is
preferred at this data scale. On WV, after recalibrating the meteorological
thresholds to the native {TIFF} data scale~$[0,0.29]$, \textsc{FlowCast}
reaches CSI$_M$~$=$~0.204 and SSIM~$=$~0.720, comparable to published
benchmarks at similar lead times.
\end{abstract}

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} satellite nowcasting, latent generative models,
flow matching, latent diffusion, variational autoencoder, perception-distortion
tradeoff, encoder representation collapse, {INSAT-3S}.

% ============================================================================
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Operational meteorological forecasting in India relies heavily on the
geostationary {INSAT-3S} satellite~\citep{insat3s}, which delivers full-disk
visible and infrared imagery at a 30-minute cadence (48~slots~per~day per
channel). Short-range nowcasting (0--6\,h) from such imagery is hard for
classical numerical weather models due to their computational cost and
resolution mismatch; data-driven generative models offer an attractive
alternative.

Two recent classes of generative models dominate the satellite/radar
nowcasting literature: \emph{flow matching}, whose continuous-time
formulation enables fast single-pass ODE
sampling~\citep{lipman2023flowmatching, flowcast2026}, and \emph{diffusion
models}, whose iterative denoising achieves state-of-the-art image quality
in many domains~\citep{ho2020ddpm, karras2022edm, rombach2022ldm}. Both
families are typically deployed in the latent space of a variational
autoencoder~\citep{kingma2014vae}, where temporal modelling is cheaper and
the decoder absorbs high-frequency reconstruction.

This work is a controlled side-by-side comparison of these two families on
a single, small, real-world dataset: 30~days of January~2026 {INSAT-3S}
imagery. We deliberately constrain ourselves to a small dataset because it
is representative of the realistic data budget for many regional
forecasting agencies, and because it exposes the inductive-bias differences
between the two model families.

The first of our two pipelines, \textsc{FlowCast}, is a faithful
re-implementation of the paper-spec Conditional Flow Matching pipeline
of~\citet{flowcast2026} with the {SEVIR}-pretrained
\texttt{AutoencoderKL} held frozen. Within this pipeline we additionally
propose and evaluate two complementary decoder-only fine-tune strategies
that target distinct failure modes of the frozen-VAE baseline. The first,
which we call \textbf{Strategy~A}, trains the decoder on real
encode-to-decode image pairs with a perceptual loss~\citep{zhang2018lpips}
in order to improve the {VAE}'s intrinsic reconstruction quality on the
INSAT domain. The second, \textbf{Strategy~B}, trains the decoder on
(predicted-latent, ground-truth-image) pairs harvested from a forward pass
of the trained forecaster, in order to additionally compensate for the
forecaster's tendency to regress toward the conditional mean during
rollout.

The second pipeline, \textsc{DiffCast}, is a deliberately more ambitious
parallel system that fine-tunes the full {VAE} (both encoder and decoder)
end-to-end on {INSAT} data and replaces the flow-matching forecaster with
an {EDM}-style latent diffusion model~\citep{karras2022edm}. Both pipelines
share the same {Earthformer-UNet} backbone~\citep{gao2022earthformer} so
that any performance difference can be attributed cleanly to the
forecaster family and the {VAE} adaptation strategy.

Beyond these two pipelines we contribute three methodological pieces that
generalise beyond this dataset. First, we introduce a \textbf{three-way
ensemble aggregation} (pixel-mean, medoid, latent-mean) that surfaces the
perception-distortion-skill tradeoff~\citep{blau2018perception} explicitly
per metric rather than collapsing the ensemble to a single image and
hiding the tradeoff inside the choice of aggregation. Second, we document
a complete \textbf{failure analysis} of \textsc{DiffCast}: although its
pixel-wise metrics surpass \textsc{FlowCast}, its predictions lose the
static geographic prior (Indian coastline, landmass) entirely, and we
trace this to an \emph{encoder representation collapse} induced by
end-to-end {VAE} fine-tuning on a small dataset. Third, we identify and
fix a \textbf{threshold-calibration bug} in the original \textsc{FlowCast}
meteorological metrics for WV brightness temperature, where default
thresholds in Kelvin~$\{220,\ldots,260\}$ do not match {INSAT}'s native
pre-normalised data range~$[0,0.29]$ and therefore yield trivially zero
{CSI} scores without our recalibration.

The headline outcome is that \textsc{FlowCast} is the better operational
choice on this small dataset, that this is for a principled reason (the
frozen encoder preserves a useful spatial prior), and that
\textsc{DiffCast}'s pixel-metric superiority is misleading. We provide all
numerical and visual evidence in \cref{sec:experiments}, and discuss the
tradeoff with reference to \citet{blau2018perception} in
\cref{sec:discussion}.

% ============================================================================
\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

\paragraph{Latent generative forecasting.} \textsc{FlowCast}, the directly
inspiring work~\citep{flowcast2026}, applies Conditional Flow
Matching~\citep{lipman2023flowmatching} to {SEVIR} radar
imagery~\citep{veillette2020sevir} using a custom 4-channel
\texttt{AutoencoderKL} with $f=8$ downsampling, trained from scratch on
{SEVIR}. Their published weights are the starting point of both pipelines
in this study. We use the same released
\texttt{b-rbmp/flowcast-cfm-sevir} checkpoint and the same backbone
architecture (Earthformer~\citep{gao2022earthformer}) so that any
performance difference can be attributed cleanly to the forecaster family
and the {VAE} adaptation strategy rather than to incidental implementation
choices.

\paragraph{Diffusion models.} {DDPM}~\citep{ho2020ddpm} introduced the
discrete-time denoising diffusion formulation; subsequent work has
substantially reduced sampling cost (e.g.\ {DDIM}~\citep{song2021ddim}) and
improved numerical conditioning. We adopt {EDM}~\citep{karras2022edm} which
unifies these advances via a continuous noise schedule with explicit
input/output preconditioning (Table~1 of that work), log-normal training
$\sigma$, and a Heun ODE sampler. This formulation has empirically the
fewest hyperparameter knobs to tune for stable training, which matters when
data is limited.

\paragraph{VAE adaptation.} The latent-diffusion line of
work~\citep{rombach2022ldm} keeps the {VAE} frozen during the diffusion
training stage. End-to-end fine-tuning of the {VAE} is occasionally done in
practice, e.g.\ to adapt the {SD-VAE} to specific domains, but is known to
risk drift; the relevant failure modes are under-discussed in the
literature. Our negative result on \textsc{DiffCast}'s {VAE} is, to our
knowledge, the first explicit documentation of \emph{encoder representation
collapse to dynamic features} on small satellite-imagery datasets.

\paragraph{Perceptual losses and metrics.} The {LPIPS}
metric~\citep{zhang2018lpips}, computed over a frozen
{VGG}~\citep{simonyan2014vgg} backbone, is widely used as a perceptual
training signal and as an evaluation metric complementary to {SSIM} and
{PSNR}. We use {LPIPS-VGG} both as a decoder fine-tuning loss
(\cref{sec:flowcast,sec:diffcast}) and as an evaluation metric.
\citet{blau2018perception} formalised the well-known tension between
distortion (e.g.\ {PSNR}) and perceptual quality: any model can be biased
toward either pole but not both simultaneously. This tension is central to
our analysis.

\paragraph{Prior nowcasting baselines.} The classical learning-based baseline
for radar/satellite nowcasting is ConvLSTM~\citep{shi2015convlstm}, which
extends LSTM to spatio-temporal data via convolutional input/forget/output
gates. Our project initially included a ConvLSTM implementation
(under \texttt{src/}) following a comparable encoder-rollout structure but
without VAE compression; this serves as a methodological starting point
that the FlowCast pipeline supersedes via two design moves: (i) compressing
images into a 4-channel latent space before modelling temporal evolution,
and (ii) replacing direct deterministic regression with a generative
conditional flow-matching objective.

\paragraph{Meteorological metrics.} Critical Success Index
(CSI)~\citep{schaefer1990csi}, Heidke Skill Score (HSS), False Alarm Ratio
(FAR), and Fractions Skill Score
(FSS)~\citep{roberts2008fss} are standard threshold-crossing scores for
operational nowcasting; they expose whether the model predicts \emph{the
right cloud cell at the right place}, which {PSNR} cannot. Continuous
Ranked Probability Score (CRPS)~\citep{matheson1976crps} measures
probabilistic calibration of the ensemble.

% ============================================================================
\section{Dataset}
\label{sec:data}

We use 30 contiguous days of {INSAT-3S} L1C single-band {TIFF} imagery
covering January~2026 over the Indian subcontinent (scene centre
$21.0^{\circ}$N, $79.0^{\circ}$E). Two channels are processed independently:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{VIS} (visible reflectance, $6.7\,\mu$m product): 1509~frames,
      stored in pre-normalised units in range $[0,\sim35]$ (1st--99th
      percentile after a robust clip).
\item \textbf{WV} (water-vapour brightness temperature, $6.7\,\mu$m
      product): 1532~frames, stored on a different native scale
      $[\sim0.007,\sim0.288]$ (\emph{not} raw Kelvin -- this miscalibration
      becomes important in \cref{sec:thresh}).
\end{itemize}

Each {TIFF} is bilinearly resized from $968\times888$ to $H\times
W=288\times256$, clipped to robust 1st/99th percentile bounds, and mapped
to $[0,1]$ before encoding (matching the {SEVIR} convention of the released
{VAE}~\citep{flowcast2026}). Norm statistics are computed once on the train
split and saved with the channel config.

Time-sorted manifests are split temporally as 80\%~train / 10\%~val /
10\%~test, preserving causality (no future-into-past leakage). VIS
additionally retains a \emph{daytime-only contiguous-runs} filter using
solar elevation~$\geq 0^{\circ}$; night slots are filled at inference with
the dataset-mean dark-fill image. The effective per-day VIS daytime window
is~$\sim22$ frames; WV is 24h continuous (48~frames per day).

% ============================================================================
\section{Methodology}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Common pipeline}
\label{sec:pipeline}
Both \textsc{FlowCast} and \textsc{DiffCast} share a four-stage structure:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Encode.} Each {TIFF} is encoded by the channel's {VAE} into
      a latent tensor $z\in\mathbb{R}^{4\times 36\times 32}$. Encoder
      output uses \texttt{latent\_dist.mode()} for determinism.
\item \textbf{Train.} A latent-space forecaster is trained on windows of
      $T_{\text{past}}+T_{\text{lead}}$ consecutive frames: VIS uses
      $5+12=17$ (daytime-only fit), WV uses $13+12=25$ (paper-spec).
\item \textbf{Roll out.} At inference, $N$~ensemble members are sampled.
      Each is rolled out autoregressively by concatenating
      $\lceil 48/T_{\text{lead}}\rceil=4$ blocks to cover a full day.
\item \textbf{Decode and aggregate.} Each member's latent trajectory is
      decoded; three aggregations are computed (\cref{sec:ensemble}).
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Latent space: pretrained \texttt{AutoencoderKL}}
\label{sec:vae}
We start from the {SEVIR}-pretrained \texttt{AutoencoderKL}~\citep{rombach2022ldm,flowcast2026},
which has the following architecture: single-channel input/output;
four \texttt{DownEncoderBlock2D} / \texttt{UpDecoderBlock2D} stages with
\texttt{block\_out\_channels} = $(128, 256, 512, 512)$;
\texttt{latent\_channels} = $4$; downsampling factor $f=8$. The model has
$\sim83.65$\,M parameters total (cf.\ \cref{sec:impl}).

The pretrained weights are inherently out-of-distribution for {INSAT}:
{SEVIR} is US weather radar over land, while {INSAT} VIS is visible
reflectance and WV is brightness temperature over a different region.
Sanity-check reconstruction (encode$\to$decode, deterministic) on
held-out val frames gives:

\begin{table}[h]
\centering\small
\caption{{VAE} roundtrip quality of the frozen {SEVIR}-pretrained
\texttt{AutoencoderKL} on {INSAT} val frames, \textbf{before any fine-tune}.}
\label{tab:vae-baseline}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Channel & Roundtrip PSNR (dB) & Roundtrip SSIM \\
\midrule
VIS & 27.27 & 0.816 \\
WV  & --    & --    \\  % left blank: not the focus
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The VIS baseline of 27.27\,dB is unexpectedly high: the encoder, despite
being out-of-distribution, preserves an adequate latent representation for
INSAT cloud imagery. This observation is critical -- it will be the
benchmark against which we measure any fine-tuning gain in
\cref{sec:flowcast,sec:diffcast}.

\subsection{\textsc{FlowCast}: frozen encoder $+$ CFM forecaster}
\label{sec:flowcast}

\subsubsection{Forecaster}
We implement the Conditional Flow Matching loss of~\citet{flowcast2026}:
given paired past latents $z_{\text{past}}\!\in\!\mathbb{R}^{B\times
T_{\text{past}}\times 4\times 36\times 32}$ and future latents
$z_{\text{fut}}$, we draw
\begin{equation}
z_0 \sim \mathcal{N}(0, I),\quad
\epsilon \sim \mathcal{N}(0, I),\quad
t \sim \mathcal{U}(0, 1),\quad
z_t = (1-t)\,z_0 + t\,z_{\text{fut}} + \sigma\epsilon
\end{equation}
with $\sigma=0.01$, and train the {Earthformer-UNet}~\citep{gao2022earthformer}
to predict the conditional velocity $u_t = z_{\text{fut}} - z_0$ by
minimising $\|v_\theta(z_t, t, z_{\text{past}}) - u_t\|_2^2$. The past is
fed via a 5-channel input (4 latent~$+$~1 mask). Sampling integrates
$\mathrm{d}z/\mathrm{d}t = v_\theta(z, t, z_{\text{past}})$ from $t=0$ to
$t=1$ with 10 Euler steps. Architectural hyperparameters are reduced from
paper-spec ($\mathit{hidden}=192,\ n\_blocks=4$) to T4-fit settings
($\mathit{hidden}=128,\ n\_blocks=2,\ \mathrm{batch}=2$) using an
auto-cap on GPUs with $<24$\,GB VRAM. Training: 100~epochs, AdamW
($\eta=5\!\times\!10^{-4}$, weight decay $10^{-4}$), cosine LR with 1\%
warmup, EMA decay 0.999, FP16 autocast.

\subsubsection{Strategy A: decoder fine-tune on real (encode--decode) pairs}
\label{sec:flowcast-A}
To improve the decoder's INSAT-domain reconstruction we freeze the encoder
and quant\_conv and unfreeze only the decoder $+$ post\_quant\_conv.
Per-batch loss:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_A = 0.1\,\|\hat{x} - x\|_2^2 \;+\; 1.0\,\mathrm{LPIPS}_{\text{VGG}}(\hat{x}, x)
\label{eq:loss-A}
\end{equation}
where $\hat{x}=\mathrm{Decoder}(\mathrm{Encoder}(x).\mathrm{mode}())$,
inputs are single-channel images in~$[0,1]$, and the LPIPS computation
uses three-channel replication and the standard~$[-1,1]$ rescaling.
LPIPS-VGG is held in FP32 to avoid the occasional NaN we observed when
running the perceptual head in mixed precision. Training: 4{,}000 steps,
AdamW $\eta=10^{-5}$, weight decay $10^{-4}$, batch 8, 200-step warmup,
cosine to $\eta=10^{-7}$.

\subsubsection{Strategy B: decoder fine-tune on (predicted-latent --
ground-truth) pairs}
\label{sec:flowcast-B}
Strategy A fixes only the VAE's intrinsic blur. To additionally compensate
for the forecaster's tendency to regress toward the conditional mean, we
generate a paired dataset of \emph{forecaster-output latents} and ground
truth images: for each training-split day, we run the trained CFM
forecaster $N_{\text{rollouts}}=4$ times and save each predicted latent
$\tilde z_{t}$ alongside its GT image $x_{t}$. This yields $\sim 2{,}100$
pairs for VIS (24~train days~$\times$~22~daytime steps~$\times$~4
rollouts). The decoder is then trained with the same loss
\cref{eq:loss-A}, but $\hat x = \mathrm{Decoder}(\tilde z_t)$ instead of
$\mathrm{Decoder}(\mathrm{Encoder}(x_t).\mathrm{mode}())$.

\paragraph{Leakage discipline (explicit assertions).} Strategy~B is
particularly vulnerable to silent train/val/test leakage because the
pair-dataset's predicted latents are themselves derived from a stochastic
forecaster rollout that consumes a seed window of past frames. A naive
construction risks two leak modes: (i)~the forecaster's input seed window
dips into val/test rows when the candidate day is near the train/val
boundary, and (ii)~the candidate day's predicted slots correspond to val
or test UTC timestamps. We mitigate both with hard assertions in
\texttt{flowcast/build\_pred\_dataset.py}:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=2pt]
\item Compute the train/val/test UTC sets from the same temporal split as
      the forecaster training: \texttt{TRAIN\_UTCS}, \texttt{VAL\_UTCS},
      \texttt{TEST\_UTCS}.
\item For each candidate day, reject if any slot or any of the $T_{\text{past}}$
      seed frames has a UTC outside \texttt{TRAIN\_UTCS}.
\item After the build completes, assert
      \texttt{set(all\_pair\_utcs).isdisjoint(VAL\_UTCS | TEST\_UTCS)} and
      same for seed UTCs. Raise on violation.
\end{enumerate}

The forecaster checkpoint SHA is also recorded in the pair-dataset
manifest so that decoder-forecaster drift cannot occur unnoticed (the
Strategy~B trainer asserts SHA-consistency at load time). After the
build, the pair-dataset manifest contains exactly $\sim 2{,}100$ pairs
($\sim$24~train days $\times$ $\sim$22~daytime slots $\times$ 4 rollouts)
totalling $\sim$19\,MB on disk (half-precision latents).

\subsection{\textsc{DiffCast}: full {VAE} fine-tune $+$ EDM diffusion forecaster}
\label{sec:diffcast}

\subsubsection{VAE: full end-to-end fine-tune}
\label{sec:diffcast-vae}
\textsc{DiffCast} unfreezes \emph{every} {VAE} parameter (encoder, decoder,
quant\_conv, post\_quant\_conv) and trains end-to-end on INSAT VIS day
frames. Per-batch loss:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\text{VAE}}^{\text{full}}
   = w_{\mathrm{MSE}}\,\|\hat x - x\|_2^2
   + w_{\mathrm{LPIPS}}\,\mathrm{LPIPS}_{\text{VGG}}(\hat x, x)
   + w_{\mathrm{KL}}\,\mathrm{KL}(q(z|x)\,\|\,\mathcal{N}(0, I))
\label{eq:loss-vae-full}
\end{equation}
where $q(z|x) = \mathcal{N}(\mu_\phi(x), \sigma_\phi(x))$ is the encoder
posterior, and $\hat x = \mathrm{Decoder}(z),\ z\sim q(z|x)$. We initially
used $w_{\mathrm{MSE}}=1.0$, $w_{\mathrm{LPIPS}}=0.5$,
$w_{\mathrm{KL}}=10^{-6}$. The very small KL weight is meant to keep the
posterior near-Gaussian without dominating reconstruction.

\paragraph{Three training failures we encountered (and the fixes).}

\begin{description}[leftmargin=*]
\item[(F0) Out-of-memory on T4 at batch size 8.]  The default
  $\mathrm{batch\_size}=8$ from \cref{eq:loss-vae-full} exceeded T4's
  14.56\,GiB even with FP16 autocast, due to the combined activation
  footprint of the encoder, decoder, the LPIPS-VGG forward (held in FP32
  for numerical stability), and the AdamW master copies of the full 83.6\,M
  trainable parameters. \textbf{Fix:} reduce batch size to 4 via the
  trainer's \texttt{--batch} CLI flag. Effective gradient noise is doubled
  but training remains stable.

\item[(F1) KL-gradient overflow under FP16 ($w_{\mathrm{KL}}\!=\!10^{-6}$).]
  The {SEVIR}-pretrained encoder produces highly confident posteriors with
  very small $\sigma_\phi(x)$. The KL term
  $\propto -\log\sigma^2$ then yields enormous values (\cref{lst:kl-fail}),
  and although $w_{\mathrm{KL}}\!=\!10^{-6}$ keeps the contribution to
  $\mathcal L$ modest, the \emph{gradient} of $-\log\sigma^2$ at small
  $\sigma$ overflows FP16. GradScaler skips a few steps, but eventually
  optimizer state goes NaN and never recovers. \textbf{Fix:} set
  $w_{\mathrm{KL}}=0$. The pretrained VAE is already KL-regularised; we
  do not need to add a fresh KL term during fine-tuning.

\begin{lstlisting}[basicstyle=\ttfamily\scriptsize,
                   frame=single, caption={Actual training log
                   showing the KL-overflow failure pattern.},
                   label=lst:kl-fail, captionpos=b]
[diffcast-vae] step  50  loss=0.3657  mse=0.02765  lpips=0.4835  kl=96343.85   <-- huge KL
[diffcast-vae] step 100  loss=nan     mse=nan      lpips=nan     kl=55148.21   <-- NaN onset
[diffcast-vae] step 150  loss=0.3190  mse=0.01035  lpips=0.4872  kl=65044.26   <-- briefly recovers
[diffcast-vae] step 250  loss=nan     mse=nan      lpips=nan     kl=12946.86   <-- permanently NaN
\end{lstlisting}

\item[(F2) FP16 decoder activation overflow on T4 (even with $w_{\mathrm{KL}}=0$).]
  Even after setting $w_{\mathrm{KL}}=0$, the decoder's intermediate
  activations at 512 inner channels overflow FP16 intermittently. Specifically
  we observed NaN re-emerging at step~100 of the post-(F1) retry, despite the
  KL term being zero-weighted. \textbf{Fix:} train in FP32 (set
  \texttt{vae\_train.fp16: false} in the channel config). This is
  $\sim 1.5$-$2\times$ slower on T4 but completes stably; a 200-step smoke
  test confirmed $+0.85$\,dB val PSNR with no NaN at any step.
\end{description}

\paragraph{The post-fix training trajectory.}  After applying (F0)+(F1)+(F2),
training is stable and the val curve is reproducible.
\cref{tab:vae-trajectory} shows the val PSNR at representative steps.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering\small
\caption{DiffCast full-VAE training results across two separate runs on
VIS (FP32, batch~4) after fixes (F0)+(F1)+(F2). The 200-step \emph{smoke
test} was a separate short run used to confirm post-fix stability before
committing the longer 5{,}000-step training. Numbers across the two runs
are not directly comparable: each starts from the same SEVIR baseline but
uses an independent RNG seed for the val subset and may select different
``best-by-val'' steps.}
\label{tab:vae-trajectory}
\begin{tabular}{llcc}
\toprule
Run                          & Step                                       & Val PSNR (dB) & Val SSIM \\
\midrule
(reference)                  & 0 (SEVIR baseline)                          & 27.27         & 0.816    \\
\midrule
Smoke test (200 steps)       & 200 (final)                                & 28.12         & 0.818    \\
\midrule
\multirow{2}{*}{Production (5{,}000 steps)}
                             & 500 (best, saved as \texttt{vae\_full.pt}) & 27.56         & 0.820    \\
                             & 5{,}000 (final)                            & 27.56         & 0.820    \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{2}{l}{30-frame held-out sanity (uses production checkpoint)} & 25.55         & 0.807    \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The production run plateaued at $+0.29$\,dB above baseline by step~500,
with later steps not improving val PSNR. The held-out 30-frame sanity
sample at 25.55\,dB is somewhat lower because it samples different days
than the per-step val subset, but is consistent with the encoder being
near its capacity ceiling for this small dataset.

\begin{negfind}
\textbf{Encoder fine-tuning produces marginal gains on small datasets.}
Despite 5{,}000 steps of full-VAE end-to-end fine-tuning on $\sim 560$
training frames, val PSNR rose from 27.27\,dB to only 27.56\,dB (+0.29~dB).
The {SEVIR}-pretrained encoder is already near-optimal in roundtrip
reconstruction for INSAT VIS; the dataset is too small to extract
substantial further signal at the encoder.
\end{negfind}

\subsubsection{EDM diffusion forecaster}
\label{sec:diffcast-edm}
We adopt the EDM formulation of \citet{karras2022edm}. Given the past
latents $z_{\text{past}}$ and the future latents $z_{\text{fut}}$, training
samples a noise level $\sigma$ log-normally:
$\log \sigma \sim \mathcal{N}(P_{\text{mean}}=-1.2,\,P_{\text{std}}^2=1.2^2)$,
forms $z_{\text{noisy}} = z_{\text{fut}} + \sigma\,\epsilon$ with
$\epsilon\!\sim\!\mathcal{N}(0,I)$, applies Karras's I/O preconditioning
(Table~1 of \citet{karras2022edm})
\begin{align}
c_{\text{skip}}(\sigma) &= \frac{\sigma_d^2}{\sigma^2 + \sigma_d^2}, &
c_{\text{out}}(\sigma)  &= \frac{\sigma \cdot \sigma_d}{\sqrt{\sigma^2 + \sigma_d^2}}, \\
c_{\text{in}}(\sigma)   &= \frac{1}{\sqrt{\sigma^2 + \sigma_d^2}}, &
c_{\text{noise}}(\sigma) &= 0.25\,\log\sigma,
\end{align}
with $\sigma_d=0.5$, and trains the same {Earthformer-UNet} backbone to
output a denoised future latent. The training loss is
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\text{EDM}}
= \mathbb{E}_{\sigma,\epsilon,z_{\text{fut}}}\!\!\left[
\lambda(\sigma)\,\| D_\theta(z_{\text{noisy}}, \sigma, z_{\text{past}}) -
z_{\text{fut}}\|_2^2 \right],
\quad
\lambda(\sigma) = \frac{\sigma^2 + \sigma_d^2}{(\sigma \cdot \sigma_d)^2}.
\end{equation}
Sampling uses the Heun ODE solver over a Karras step-size schedule with
$N=18$ steps, $\sigma_{\min}=0.002$, $\sigma_{\max}=80$, $\rho=7$
(\citet{karras2022edm}~Algorithm~1). Other training settings mirror
\textsc{FlowCast} (100~epochs, AdamW, cosine~LR with 1\% warmup, EMA~0.999,
FP16 autocast, T4 auto-cap). The same mask-channel concatenation is used
to distinguish past and future frames in the input.

\subsection{Three-way ensemble aggregation}
\label{sec:ensemble}
A stochastic forecaster ($N$ ensemble members) admits multiple ways to
collapse to a single per-slot image; each implicitly trades sharpness for
accuracy. We compute all three and report metrics separately:

\begin{description}[leftmargin=*]
\item[\textsc{pixmean}.] $\hat x_{\text{px}} = \tfrac{1}{N}\sum_{n=1}^N \hat
  x^{(n)}$. Minimises pixel-wise variance; best PSNR/CRPS; visually blurry
  because averaging across diverse cloud realisations smears edges.
\item[\textsc{medoid}.] $\hat x_{\text{med}} = \hat x^{(n^*)}$ where
  $n^* = \arg\min_n \|\hat x^{(n)} - \hat x_{\text{px}}\|_2$. Selects the
  single trajectory closest to the ensemble mean; visually crisp because
  it is a single, plausible cloud realisation rather than an average.
\item[\textsc{latmean}.] $\hat x_{\text{lat}} = \mathrm{Decoder}\!\bigl(
  \tfrac{1}{N}\sum_n \tilde z^{(n)}\bigr)$. Average in latent space, decode
  once. Intermediate sharpness; the decoder partially recovers texture
  that pixel-mean would have smoothed away.
\end{description}

This three-way decomposition surfaces the
perception-distortion tradeoff~\citep{blau2018perception} explicitly: in
all our experiments, $\textsc{pixmean}$ has the highest PSNR while
$\textsc{medoid}$ has the most visually defensible cloud structure;
\textsc{latmean} typically falls between the two on PSNR.

\subsection{Evaluation protocol}
\label{sec:metrics}

We score on the channel's last test-split day (2026-01-15 by default; we
discuss multi-date generalisation in \cref{sec:limits}). For each
ensemble mode (pixmean/medoid/latmean) we compute:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{PSNR}, \textbf{SSIM}~\citep{wang2004ssim}, MSE -- pixel-level
      reconstruction fidelity. PSNR is computed in physical units with
      $\mathrm{data\_range} = \mathit{high}-\mathit{low}$ from the channel's
      norm stats.
\item \textbf{CSI$_M$, HSS$_M$, FAR$_M$} -- threshold-crossing categorical
      skill, averaged over a channel-appropriate set of thresholds
      (\cref{sec:thresh}). CSI~\citep{schaefer1990csi}, HSS, and FAR are
      computed pointwise from the $2\times2$ contingency table.
\item \textbf{CSI$_M$-P16, FSS$_M$-P16}~\citep{roberts2008fss} -- pooled
      spatial variants computed over non-overlapping $16\times16$
      neighbourhoods, more robust to small location errors.
\item \textbf{CRPS}~\citep{matheson1976crps} -- Gaussian closed-form CRPS
      using the full $N$-member ensemble (independent of aggregation
      choice), normalised by $\mathrm{data\_range}$.
\end{itemize}

For VIS, night slots are excluded from all metrics (the forecaster never
sees night frames, and the displayed dark-fill image would trivially
inflate or deflate scores depending on cloud activity at those latitudes).

\subsection{Threshold calibration for WV}
\label{sec:thresh}

The \textsc{FlowCast} reference~\citep{flowcast2026} uses Kelvin
brightness-temperature thresholds $\{220, 230, 240, 250, 260\}$\,K to
identify cold cloud tops. We observed that the {INSAT-3S} WV {TIFF} data
is in a pre-normalised scale spanning empirically $[0.007,\,0.288]$, not
raw Kelvin. With the original Kelvin thresholds, $p > \theta$ is
\emph{never} true for any pixel, so CSI, HSS, and FAR are trivially zero
and FSS is trivially one. The metrics are entirely uninformative.

We recomputed channel-appropriate thresholds from the empirical distribution
of the WV pool over 200 frames:

\begin{table}[h]
\centering\small
\caption{WV brightness-temperature distribution (sampled 200 frames),
informing the threshold recalibration. Percentiles are on the native
TIFF data scale, \emph{not} Kelvin.}
\label{tab:wv-distribution}
\begin{tabular}{lc|lc}
\toprule
Statistic & Value & Statistic & Value\\
\midrule
$\min$    & 0.007 & $\mathrm{P}_{50}$ & 0.186 \\
$\max$    & 0.288 & $\mathrm{P}_{75}$ & 0.231 \\
$\mathrm{mean}$ & 0.181 & $\mathrm{P}_{90}$ & 0.252 \\
$\mathrm{P}_{1}$  & 0.058 & $\mathrm{P}_{95}$ & 0.260 \\
$\mathrm{P}_{25}$ & 0.134 & $\mathrm{P}_{99}$ & 0.271 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

We replace the Kelvin thresholds with $\{0.20, 0.225, 0.25, 0.265, 0.28\}$,
corresponding approximately to the $55$th, $72$nd, $89$th, $97$th, and
$99.5$th percentiles -- upper-tail values where an ``event'' (e.g.\ a high
WV / cold-cloud signature) is rare but predictable. VIS thresholds
$\{5,10,15,20,25,30\}$ (on its native albedo scale) require no change.

\begin{finding}
\textbf{Default reference thresholds may not transfer between datasets
without empirical recalibration.} On WV, using
Kelvin-scaled thresholds produced uniformly trivial CSI~$=0$, masking the
real meteorological skill of the forecaster. Recomputing thresholds from
the data percentiles raised CSI$_M$ to a meaningful 0.204 with no other
change.
\end{finding}

