\textsc{DiffCast} attains $+3.23$\,dB PSNR and $-0.090$ CRPS over
\textsc{FlowCast} Strategy~B on the pixel-mean aggregation
(15.25 vs.\ 12.02), but its CSI$_M$ collapses to 0.012 (vs.\ 0.064
for \textsc{FlowCast} Strategy~B) -- a $5\!\times$ drop. The decoder-only
fine-tune (Strategy~B in this table) produces results essentially
indistinguishable from the frozen-decoder \textsc{FlowCast} baseline at
the top of the table -- the differences are within the CFM stochastic
sampling noise we documented above ($N=8$ vs.\ $N=32$ flat-metric
analysis). This is consistent with our intermediate finding that the
\textsc{SEVIR}-pretrained \texttt{AutoencoderKL} was already near its
INSAT VIS capacity ceiling: neither decoder-only nor full-VAE fine-tuning
produced large reconstruction gains on this dataset.

Increasing \textsc{FlowCast}'s ensemble size from $N=8$ to $N=32$ does
\emph{not} measurably improve PSNR ($\Delta=-0.10$\,dB) or any other metric.
This is unexpected ($\sqrt{32/8}\!\approx\!2\times$ variance reduction
predicts $\sim$+1\,dB) and indicates that ensemble variance is not the
PSNR bottleneck -- the forecaster's $N=8$ predictions already concentrate
around the same conditional-mean trajectory. The variation across runs is
instead dominated by which sample wins the medoid selection: across our
runs \texttt{medoid\_idx} took values $\{4, 5, 6, 7\}$ on different
$N\!=\!8$ rollouts, with all medoid trajectories appearing visually
similar -- further evidence of effective mode-collapse on this small
dataset.

