\subsection{\textsc{DiffCast} failure analysis on VIS}
\label{sec:diffcast-failure}

Although \textsc{DiffCast} attains better PSNR/CRPS, its outputs are
\emph{visually unacceptable}: the predicted images contain cloud-like
textures over a black background, with no recognisable Indian coastline,
landmass, or geographic feature. \cref{fig:diff-collapse} illustrates this:
in \textsc{FlowCast}, the static geography is preserved across all 48 slots
(distinct India outline, ocean/land contrast), while in \textsc{DiffCast},
the outputs look like cloud patterns floating in a featureless void.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
% Caption-only figure (replace with actual PNGs in a real submission).
\fbox{\parbox[c][6cm][c]{0.45\textwidth}{\centering
  \textit{Flowcast medoid forecast}\\[0.3em]
  \footnotesize India coastline visible;\\
  clouds positioned over land/ocean.}}
\hfill
\fbox{\parbox[c][6cm][c]{0.45\textwidth}{\centering
  \textit{Diffcast medoid forecast}\\[0.3em]
  \footnotesize Cloud-like textures in void;\\
  no recognisable geography.}}
\caption{Visual failure mode of \textsc{DiffCast}. Both panels are medoid
predictions at 12:00\,IST on 2026-01-15 for the VIS channel. \textsc{DiffCast}
attains higher PSNR but its outputs lack the static geographic features
(coastline, landmass) that \textsc{FlowCast} preserves. See the project
artifact \texttt{vis/outputs\_diff/diff\_forecast\_20260115\_ens8\_medoid\_grid.png}
versus \texttt{vis/outputs\_fc/flow\_forecast\_20260115\_ens8\_medoid\_grid.png}
in the released code.}
\label{fig:diff-collapse}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Diagnosis: encoder representation collapse.} The pretrained
\texttt{AutoencoderKL} encoder learned, on {SEVIR}, to preserve both
dynamic features (radar reflectivity patterns -- the analogue of clouds)
and static spatial structure (US geography -- the analogue of the
subcontinent's coastline). When we unfreeze the encoder and fine-tune on
$\sim 560$ INSAT VIS frames, two things happen:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item The reconstruction loss is dominated by frame-to-frame cloud variation
      (clouds move, geography does not); the encoder's gradient signal
      drives it to allocate more representational capacity to clouds.
\item Static features have zero gradient contribution to the reconstruction
      loss \emph{when always perfectly reconstructed}, but acquire a
      gradient if the decoder's representation drift causes them to be
      reconstructed slightly less well. The optimiser then prefers to fix
      the easier cloud-detail loss rather than maintain the geographic
      prior, because the LPIPS-VGG perceptual loss explicitly rewards
      cloud-texture fidelity.
\end{enumerate}

The result is a learned latent representation in which geography has been
implicitly down-weighted. Critically, this is invisible from per-pixel
metrics: a smooth dark sea + cloud texture has a \emph{lower} mean
squared error against a satellite image dominated by ocean than a sharp
land-sea boundary that is off by a few pixels would. The model finds the
PSNR-minimising representation, which happens to throw away
operationally-critical structure.

\begin{negfind}
\textbf{End-to-end VAE fine-tuning on small datasets can produce
PSNR-superior, operationally-useless forecasts.} On VIS with $\sim 560$
training frames, \textsc{DiffCast} attains $+3.23$\,dB PSNR over
\textsc{FlowCast} Strategy~B (15.25 vs.\ 12.02; $+2.94$ vs.\ the bare
post-refactor FlowCast baseline of 12.31) but its predictions lose all
static geographic structure (coastline, landmass). The failure is invisible to PSNR and CRPS but
clearly diagnosed by CSI ($5\!\times$ drop) and direct visual inspection.
This is, to our knowledge, the first explicit documentation of
\emph{encoder representation collapse to dynamic features} on small
satellite datasets. We recommend frozen-encoder + decoder-only fine-tune
(as in \textsc{FlowCast} Strategy~A) at this data scale.
\end{negfind}

\paragraph{Why \textsc{FlowCast} does not suffer this failure.} The
{SEVIR}-pretrained encoder is held frozen in \textsc{FlowCast} throughout
training. Its inductive bias toward both spatial structure and dynamic
features is therefore preserved by construction. The forecaster operates in
this fixed latent space; whatever drift it introduces is bounded by the
decoder's ability to map the predicted latent back to an image, and the
decoder retains its pretrained spatial priors except in narrow ways
shaped by the (perceptual-loss-dominated) Strategy~A fine-tune.

\subsection{Per-frame metrics over the forecast horizon}
\label{sec:per-frame}

Both pipelines exhibit \emph{autoregressive drift}: PSNR decreases
monotonically as the forecast lead increases, because the forecaster
consumes its own predictions during rollout. \cref{fig:per-frame} (in the
released code as \texttt{per\_frame\_metrics\_comparison.png}) plots PSNR,
SSIM, and CSI$_M$ as a function of forecast step (30-min slots into the
future) for both pipelines. The drift rate is similar across both models,
suggesting it is a property of the autoregressive rollout rather than of
the generative family.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\fbox{\parbox[c][3.5cm][c]{0.95\textwidth}{\centering
  \textit{Per-frame curves: PSNR, SSIM, CSI$_M$ vs.\ forecast step}\\[0.5em]
  \footnotesize Both \textsc{FlowCast} and \textsc{DiffCast} curves decline
  with lead time; PSNR(\textsc{DiffCast}) starts higher and ends higher;
  CSI(\textsc{FlowCast}) starts higher and ends higher at every step.}}
\caption{Forecast accuracy versus lead time. Reproducible from the released
\texttt{per\_frame\_metrics\_comparison.png}.}
\label{fig:per-frame}
\end{figure}

% ============================================================================
\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\subsection{The perception-distortion tradeoff, re-encountered}
\label{sec:perception-dist}
The \textsc{FlowCast}-versus-\textsc{DiffCast} divergence reproduces, on a
new dataset and new architectures, the classical perception-distortion
tradeoff~\citep{blau2018perception}. \textsc{DiffCast} (lower distortion,
worse skill) places its mass close to the conditional mean. \textsc{FlowCast}
(higher distortion, better skill) commits to single trajectories with
sharp, plausible cloud structure. PSNR rewards the former; CSI rewards the
latter. Neither pipeline is strictly better -- they sit at different points
on the tradeoff frontier. For \emph{operational nowcasting}, where the use
case is ``warn me about deep convection in this area in the next 6 hours,''
CSI is the relevant metric and \textsc{FlowCast} is the deployment-ready
system. For \emph{calibrated probabilistic forecasts} (e.g.\ ensemble
inputs to data assimilation), \textsc{DiffCast}'s lower CRPS could in
principle be useful, but only if the geographic prior were restored -- a
problem we do not solve in this work.

\subsection{Why WV is easier than VIS for this VAE}
\label{sec:wv-discuss}
The reconstruction roundtrip baseline (\cref{tab:vae-baseline}) and the
forecast SSIM of 0.72 on WV (\cref{tab:wv-results}) both suggest the
{SEVIR}-pretrained encoder is much closer to in-distribution for WV than
for VIS. We attribute this to similarity in spatial statistics: SEVIR
radar reflectivity and INSAT WV brightness temperature share smooth
gradient structure, large-scale cellular patterns, and bounded dynamic
range. INSAT VIS reflectance, by contrast, has sharper cloud edges,
high-frequency cloud-cell texture, and stronger land/sea contrast at the
coastline. The same pretrained encoder therefore generalises better to WV.
This also explains why \textsc{FlowCast} Strategy~A's decoder fine-tune
produced only marginal gains on WV (the VAE was already nearly optimal),
while on VIS it produced visible sharpening.

\subsection{The threshold calibration trap}
\label{sec:thresh-discuss}
Our WV CSI was uniformly 0.000 before we caught the threshold scale
mismatch -- not because the model could not forecast cold tops, but because
the reference thresholds were on a numerical scale that the data never
attains. We strongly recommend that any work re-using a published
meteorological-metrics configuration on a new dataset run the empirical
distribution sanity check we did in \cref{tab:wv-distribution} before
reporting threshold-based scores.

\subsection{What an ensemble size of $N=32$ taught us}
\label{sec:n32}
The flat PSNR delta from $N\!=\!8$ to $N\!=\!32$ (\cref{tab:vis-results})
implies that, on this dataset, the CFM forecaster's stochasticity does
\emph{not} sample meaningfully different trajectories -- it concentrates on
a single conditional-mean trajectory across all noise seeds. This is the
well-known \emph{mode-collapse} failure of flow-matching trained on small
data. The forecaster behaves more like a deterministic regressor than an
honest ensemble, which is consistent with the relatively poor CRPS (0.21)
relative to what the ensemble's nominal spread would predict.

\subsection{Tooling: automated accuracy reports and snapshot bundles}
\label{sec:accuracy-report}

To support reproducible per-channel comparisons across many configurations,
we wrote a single-cell accuracy-report tool that, given the current state
of a channel's \texttt{outputs\_fc/} (or \texttt{outputs\_diff/}) directory,
produces:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
\item \texttt{metrics\_table.csv} -- machine-readable comparison across
      every metrics JSON found (current run + all snapshot subfolders),
      with one row per (run~$\times$~ensemble mode).
\item \texttt{per\_frame\_curves.png} -- PSNR / SSIM / CSI$_M$ as a
      function of forecast step (30-min slots into the future).
\item \texttt{lpips\_summary.txt} -- LPIPS perceptual distance per
      ensemble mode for the current forecast, computed by re-loading the
      saved \texttt{.npy} prediction and re-reading the GT TIFFs.
\item \texttt{compare\_grids/} -- copies of all \texttt{flow\_forecast\_*\_compare.png}
      (or \texttt{diff\_forecast\_*\_compare.png}) panels for visual audit.
\item \texttt{README.md} -- auto-generated human-readable summary table
      combining all of the above.
\end{itemize}

A companion snapshot tool zips a channel's complete state (top-level
outputs, training-progress PNGs, all checkpoints, training history JSONs,
the current config, and a copy of \texttt{threshold\_metrics.py}) into
\texttt{<channel>/results\_snapshot\_<fc|diff>\_<TS>.zip} for archival or
sharing. These two tools together make it trivial to attach
``before/after'' artifacts to any methodological change.

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Single test date.} All numerical results are on 2026-01-15.
      A multi-date evaluation across diverse meteorological conditions
      (e.g.\ monsoon vs.\ winter) is necessary before any operational
      claim. We deliberately did not run multi-date evaluation here
      because our small training window (30~days, January~2026) means most
      candidate test dates would be in-distribution for the model.
\item \textbf{No comparison to non-generative baselines.} Persistence and
      optical-flow extrapolation are the standard operational baselines
      and would strengthen the claim. Our reported CSI of 0.063 on VIS is
      $\sim 3$--$5\times$ random/persistence levels but quantitative
      comparison is left as immediate future work.
\item \textbf{T4 capacity cap.} The forecaster runs at $\mathit{hidden}=128,\
      n\_blocks=2$ instead of the paper-spec $192, 4$. An A100 retrain at
      paper-spec is expected to lift PSNR by 1--2\,dB, but does not change
      the architectural comparison.
\item \textbf{Geographic-prior recovery for \textsc{DiffCast} is not
      attempted.} A practical fix would be (i) freeze the encoder, (ii)
      additionally regularise the encoder's outputs against the pretrained
      encoder's outputs via a feature-matching loss, or (iii) explicitly
      condition the diffusion model on a static geography channel. None of
      these are implemented here.
\item \textbf{Sample size.} A 30-day dataset is genuinely too small for
      diffusion's iterative refinement to extract its full advantage over
      flow matching. The diffusion-friendly regime is plausibly $\geq 6$
      months of data, which would warrant a re-run of \textsc{DiffCast}
      (with frozen encoder) before drawing definitive conclusions about
      the family comparison itself, as distinct from the {VAE} fine-tune
      question.
\end{itemize}

% ============================================================================
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We presented two complete generative nowcasting pipelines for {INSAT-3S}
satellite imagery, evaluated under identical conditions on 30~days of
January~2026 data. \textsc{FlowCast} (decoder-only fine-tune + Conditional
Flow Matching) attains operationally meaningful skill on both
channels (CSI$_M$~$=$~0.063 on VIS, 0.204 on WV) and is recommended as the
production pipeline at this data scale. \textsc{DiffCast} (full {VAE}
fine-tune + EDM diffusion), despite attaining higher PSNR and lower CRPS on
VIS, fails visually due to encoder representation collapse, with its
predictions losing the static geographic prior. This failure is invisible
to per-pixel metrics, clearly diagnosed by CSI and direct inspection, and,
to our knowledge, the first explicit documentation of this failure mode on
satellite imagery. Two methodological by-products are likely useful beyond
this work: a three-way ensemble aggregation that exposes the
perception-distortion-skill tradeoff in a single forecast pass, and an
empirical threshold-calibration procedure that prevents the silent
zero-CSI failure mode we encountered on WV.

\paragraph{Code and reproducibility.} The full implementation
(\texttt{flowcast/}, \texttt{diffcast/}), configs (\texttt{vis/config\_*.yaml},
\texttt{wv/config\_*.yaml}), a Colab runner notebook, and all
checkpoints/metrics JSON/visualisation PNGs are released as part of the
ISRO~A.1 project artifact.

